 ],
 "literal": "Thorny-legged shrimp's bent-backed large swarm / ocean's crashing wave brings, the lord of the shore / when they united, even on the day / like this indeed, alas, was the beautiful forehead's beauty!",
 "translation": "O lord of the maritime shore, where the crashing waves of the ocean bring large swarms of bent-backed, thorny-legged shrimp! Even on the days when you were united with her, the beauty of her lovely forehead was just like this—pale and troubled with the fear of your departure.",
 "modern": "முட்கள் போன்ற கால்களையும் வளைந்த முதுகையும் உடைய இறால்களின் பெரிய கூட்டத்தை, கடலின் கரையை நோக்கி வீசும் அலைகள் கொண்டு வந்து சேர்க்கும் நெய்தல் நிலத் தலைவனே! நீ அவளோடு கூடி இருந்த நாட்களிலும் கூட, அவளுடைய அழகான நெற்றியின் அழகு இப்படித்தான் பிரிவை அஞ்சிப் பசலை படர்ந்து இருந்தது!",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Neytal (seashore) landscape, where the heroine's friend (toḻi) addresses the hero (tuṟaivaṉ). She points out that the heroine's suffering is not new; even during their moments of union, her forehead lost its luster and looked pale because she was constantly anxious about his impending departure. The natural imagery of the crashing waves bringing a large swarm of bent-backed, thorny-legged shrimp to the shore mirrors the heroine's state. Just as the relentless waves push the shrimp onto the shore, the constant thoughts of separation crash upon her mind, leaving her bent with grief even when they are together. Key words that carry this emotion are 'puṇariya irunta ñāṉṟum' (even on the days they were united) and 'iṉṉatu' (like this, pale and anxious).",
